# ping6 — real-world examples

## Discover all IPv6 neighbors via multicast ping

```sh
# ff02::1 is the all-nodes link-local multicast address; every IPv6 host on the segment replies
ping6 -I eth0 ff02::1
```

## Ping a link-local address (interface is required)

```sh
# Link-local (fe80::/10) addresses are ambiguous without an interface
ping6 -I eth0 fe80::1a2b:3cff:fe4d:5e6f

# Alternative zone-id syntax
ping6 fe80::1a2b:3cff:fe4d:5e6f%eth0
```

## Check IPv6 connectivity from the homelab

```sh
# Verify mediaserver answers over IPv6
ping6 -c 4 mediaserver

# Test global IPv6 reachability
ping6 -c 4 ipv6.google.com

# Larger payload and 0.5s interval to spot flaky links
ping6 -c 20 -i 0.5 -s 1400 homelab
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-I <iface>` | Interface to send from (required for link-local/multicast) |
| `-c <n>` | Stop after n packets |
| `-i <sec>` | Interval between packets |
| `-s <bytes>` | Payload size |
| `-w <sec>` | Overall deadline |

> Note: on modern systems `ping6` is often a symlink for `ping -6`.
